---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import NnMasthead from '../../../components/NnMasthead.astro';
import race from '../../../content/race.json';

// The same event theme as the rest of Nightingale Nightmare — see
// packages/shared/styles/nn-theme.css.
//
// **This is the page with the most horror on it and the least horror in it.** Everything
// below the hero is a time, an address or an instruction, and every one of them is ink on
// white in Inter. The schedule in particular is never set in the display face: somebody
// reads it on a phone, outdoors, in November, deciding whether they are late.
import '@src/shared/styles/nn-theme.css';

// The times, the address and the prize categories are `race.json`'s rather than this
// page's, for the reason the file exists: a committee correcting the briefing time should
// edit one line of one file, not search four pages of markup for it.
//
// **What is deliberately absent is as considered as what is here.** The mockup this
// campaign is drawn from carries an entry price, a deadline for passing a place on, an
// entry-opening time and a live places-remaining counter. The price and the window are the
// database's — see `/nn/`'s note — and the other two are simply not decided: the 2023 dates
// are a pattern, not a commitment anybody has made for 2026.
//
// **The clocks joke is not reusable and is not here.** The 2023 copy makes it twice — "an
// extended witching hour the night before" and "remember the clocks change… so no excuse
// for lateness". It was true that year, when the race fell on the transition day. In 2026
// the clocks go back on 25 October and this race is 1 November, a week later and squarely
// in GMT. `site.spec.ts` asserts the phrase appears on none of the three content pages.
//
// **The 2023 instructions were a pre-race email and are not page copy.** They open
// "Commiserations on entering the Nightingale Nightmare 10k" and sign off "next Sunday
// morning" — written to people who had already entered, days out. This page is read by
// somebody deciding whether to enter, months out, so nothing here assumes either.
//
// See docs/architecture/principles.md#stop-and-ask
const canonical = 'https://new.southvillerunningclub.co.uk/nn/2026/race-day/';

// **The start is not at race HQ, and that is the one fact on this page somebody most needs
// on the morning.** The club's 2023 event profile carried it exactly once, as a `goo.gl`
// shortlink — and Google has been retiring those, so the single most important direction
// this race gives out depended on a redirect the club does not own, cannot renew, and would
// not be told about the day it stopped resolving.
//
// **So `race.json` stores the coordinates and this file builds the link.** Resolved from
// that shortlink while it still worked: 51.4468588, -2.6250503. A pin is a fact about the
// world and survives its hosting; a shortlink is a fact about a third party's database.
// If Google's URL shape ever changes, one line here changes and the coordinates do not.
//
// The numbers are printed on the page as well as linked, because a coordinate pair is
// usable in any map application, offline, and read aloud down a phone — and because a link
// that fails should not take the answer with it.
//
// **No embedded map**, deliberately: an iframe would put a third-party tracker on a club
// page nobody has assessed, on the page most likely to be opened on race morning.
const startFinish = `${race.startFinish.lat}, ${race.startFinish.lng}`;
const startFinishMap = `https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=${race.startFinish.lat},${race.startFinish.lng}`;
---

<Base
  title="Race day — Nightingale Nightmare"
  description="Where Nightingale Nightmare starts from, when everything happens on the day, and what is at race HQ."
  canonical={canonical}
  theme="nn"
>
  <NnMasthead slot="masthead" />

  <header class="nn-hero nn-hero-compact">
    <div class="nn-wrap">
      <h1>Race day</h1>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="nn-wrap">
    <div class="nn-card nn-prose">
      <p class="lede">
        {race.date}. The race starts at {race.startTime}, and the morning before it has
        rather more in it than that.
      </p>

      <h2>Race HQ</h2>

      <p><strong>{race.location}</strong></p>

      <p>
        Everything that is not the race itself happens here: registration, the briefing,
        your bag, and the cake afterwards. There is <strong>free parking on site</strong>,
        along with toilets, shelter, bag drop and changing areas.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Please lift-share if you can.</strong> The car park is large, but it is not
        infinite, and there is no on-street parking for this race — people live on those streets,
        and they did not enter it. Anything worth having is worth locking in the car rather
        than leaving on a seat.
      </p>

      {
        /* **The start is a different place from HQ, and it gets its own heading for that
        reason.** The two have been confused in the club's own materials before, which is
        why this section names the distinction in its first sentence rather than leaving it
        to be inferred from an address further down.

        The coordinates are printed as well as linked — see the note at the head of this
        file on why they are stored rather than a shortlink. */
      }
      <h2>The start and the finish</h2>

      {
        /* **No distance from HQ is stated, and that is not an oversight.** Nobody has
        supplied one. It is tempting to derive it — there are coordinates for the start
        right here — but race HQ is an address rather than a pin, and a figure worked out
        from a guessed school entrance is exactly the plausible-looking number this
        repository keeps out of markup. What the schedule already says is enough to act on:
        be at HQ, and leave with everybody else at 10:30. */
      }
      <p>
        <strong>Not at race HQ.</strong> The race starts and finishes away from the school,
        and everybody walks down together at 10:30 — so on the day there is nothing to find
        on your own, and the walk is on the schedule below rather than left to you.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you would like to see it beforehand, it is at{' '}
        <span class="nn-mono">{startFinish}</span> —{' '}
        <a href={startFinishMap} rel="noopener">show it on a map</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The morning, in order</h2>

      {
        /* A description list rather than a table: each row is one time and one thing, with
        no second column of data to line up. It is also what degrades best at 320px, where a
        two-column table either scrolls sideways or squeezes the words to one per line.

        **The `<div>` around each pair is deliberate and is valid inside a `<dl>`.** It is
        what lets a row be a grid — time in its own column, wrapping to a stacked row on a
        narrow screen — without the two halves of a pair drifting apart in the markup. */
      }
      <dl class="nn-schedule">
        {
          race.schedule.map(({ time, what }) => (
            <div class="nn-schedule-row">
              <dt>{time}</dt>
              <dd>{what}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <p>
        <strong>There is a bag drop at HQ and another one at the start</strong>, so a coat
        you want at 10:30 does not have to be a coat you abandon at 11:00.
      </p>

      <h2>Fancy dress</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>Strongly encouraged</strong>, and that goes for anyone who comes to watch
        you as well. It is a Halloween race and it looks like one.
      </p>

      <p>
        Judging is from a photograph rather than from the course: collect your number,
        then go upstairs at HQ and have your picture taken. No photograph, no prize —
        which is the only rule the competition has.
      </p>

      <h2>Afterwards, at HQ</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>Tea, coffee, hot chocolate and cake</strong>, all free, and the cake is
        baked by club volunteers rather than bought. There is a donation tin out for a
        local charity if you would like to put something in it.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Please stay and cheer the rest of them in.</strong> The people still out there
        at your finish time are having a harder morning than you are, and a finish line with
        nobody at it is a bleak thing to run towards. Bring something warm and dry to change
        into and it is a much easier promise to keep.
      </p>

      <h2>Prizes</h2>

      {
        /* **A grid rather than the schedule's list, because these are not in an order.**
        The morning is a sequence and reads down; the prizes are four independent things
        and read as tiles. Still a `<dl>` — each is a term and its detail — with a `<div>`
        per pair, which is valid inside a `<dl>` and is what makes a tile.

        Which one is yellow is `highlight` in `race.json` rather than a class written here,
        so moving the emphasis is a committee edit and not a CSS change. */
      }
      <dl class="nn-prizes">
        {
          race.prizes.map(({ category, detail, highlight }) => (
            <div class:list={['nn-prize', highlight && 'nn-prize-highlight']}>
              <dt>{category}</dt>
              <dd>{detail}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <p class="nn-medal">{race.finisherPrize}</p>

      <p>
        Prizegiving is at HQ once the last finisher is in. Standing around for it is the
        polite thing to do and usually the more entertaining one.
      </p>
    </div>

    {
      /* The same call to action as the other two content pages — see the note on
    course.astro for why it is not on `/nn/`. */
    }
    {
      /* **It points at this year's page rather than at the interest form**, and it no longer
      says entries are shut. Whether they are is decided by a row and read at serve time; a
      static page cannot know it, and the sentence that used to be here becomes false the
      morning entries open — silently, on a page somebody is reading to decide whether to
      enter. The year page answers the question properly, in whichever state it is in. */
    }
    <section class="nn-panel">
      <h2>The 2026 race</h2>
      <p class="nn-panel-note">{race.date} · {race.startTime} · {race.distance}</p>
      <a class="nn-cta" href="/nn/2026/">The date, the facts, and how to enter</a>
    </section>

    <footer>
      <p>
        Southville Running Club. The full list of race facts, including anything still to
        be confirmed, is on <a href="/nn/2026/">this year's race page</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Questions? <a href={`mailto:${race.contact}`}>{race.contact}</a>
      </p>
    </footer>
  </div>

  {
    /* The same decorative artwork as the other Nightingale Nightmare pages, and empty for
    the same reason — it is a CSS background. See the artwork section of nn-theme.css. */
  }
  <div class="nn-hands" aria-hidden="true"></div>
</Base>
